211: 
212: ## Frontend type boundaries
213: 
214: Route inputs are parsed into closed Rust types before strategic logic runs.
215: Character-session IDs, location kinds, quest and mission states, queued party
216: actions, and inventory transfer entries do not remain arbitrary strings. Queued
217: party actions serialize a tagged `PartyAction` enum; approval reconstructs the
218: reducer call from that variant instead of replaying an arbitrary reducer name
219: and positional JSON arguments.
220: 
221: Cross-feature route support is organized under `routes/data.rs`,
222: `routes/inventory_forms.rs`, `routes/party_actions.rs`, and `routes/travel.rs`.
223: Shared component CSS, strategic-location CSS, and final utility overrides live
224: in separate files while preserving their cascade order.
225: Database transport or row-decoding failures must remain distinct from a
226: successful empty query and should produce an explicit unavailable response or
227: logged error state.
228: 
229: ## Geographic map presentation
230: 
231: Settlement maps use the persistent strategic Bevy renderer and its fullscreen
232: canvas. The server supplies observer-admitted canonical place identities,
233: geographic coordinates and the selected route; Bevy projects the corresponding
234: HTML destination links. Only exactly believed or visited case sites are
235: admitted. Their public display title remains distinct from the private name.
236: Existing route inspection, provisioning and travel submission remain server
237: workflows.
238: 
239: The map's browser owner retains four recent terrain windows and one installed
240: window per source and home. Closing a map cancels pending HTTP work and preserves
241: the renderer's camera pose and GPU terrain. Camera gestures send semantic
242: commands; JavaScript does not perform another geographic projection. The
243: compositor reveals a map only after its admitted terrain window is presented
244: and its current overlay revision settles. Navigation cannot expose a previous
245: observer's overlay. Terrain errors require
246: an explicit retry rather than issuing requests every frame.
247: 
248: Startup loads the native final terrain pack used by route planning; there is no
249: raster tile loader or tile route. Geographic HTTP products remain read-only and
250: do not prepare settlement scenes. Roads and a single focused city exterior are
251: subsequent refinements. The native pack, renderer assets and initial strategic
252: environment still contribute to startup time. Keeping their owner persistent
253: allows later loading improvements without replacing this interface contract.
