1: //! Where every small plate of a piece lies, decided on its base fit.
2: //!
3: //! Each grid is covered by rows of plates from its top down, as many as its
4: //! length along the piece takes at the plate's overlap. Each row takes as
5: //! many plates as fit across it at that height -- whole ones across an open
6: //! plate, evenly spaced ones around a closed one -- every other row shifted
7: //! by the stagger. A plate is tilted along the piece so its top tucks under
8: //! the row above and its foot lies over the row below, the rows clearing
9: //! each other by the lacing's room however many there are.
10: //!
11: //! A plate keeps its grid position on every morph of the piece, so the
12: //! plates correspond wherever the body moves the surface.
